### Primary Module Interface Unit

A primary module interface unit declares the module and exports its public API. There can only be **one** primary module interface per module name.

```cpp
// math.cppm (or math.ixx)
export module math;

// Publicly visible to all importers
export namespace math {
    [[nodiscard]] constexpr int add(int a, int b) noexcept {
        return a + b;
    }

    export class Matrix {
    public:
        explicit Matrix(int rows, int cols);
        void resize(int rows, int cols);
    private:
        int rows_;
        int cols_;
    };
}
```

### Module Implementation Unit

Implementation units contain definitions not needed in the interface. They automatically import the primary module interface (everything in the module interface is visible, even non-exported items).

```cpp
// math_impl.cpp
module math; // Note: NO 'export'

namespace math {
    Matrix::Matrix(int rows, int cols) : rows_(rows), cols_(cols) {}
    void Matrix::resize(int rows, int cols) {
        rows_ = rows;
        cols_ = cols;
    }
}
```

### Private Module Fragment (`module :private;`)

Enables keeping interface and implementation in a single physical file without triggering re-compilation of importers when the implementation changes. Importers only depend on the interface before `:private;`.

```cpp
// single_file_logger.cppm
export module logger;

import std;

export class Logger {
public:
    void log(std::string_view msg);
};

// Anything below this line is NOT part of the interface BMI.
// Changing implementation code below does not cause importers to recompile.
module :private;

void Logger::log(std::string_view msg) {
    std::println("[LOG] {}", msg);
}
```

*Constraint:* A module with a private module fragment cannot have any other module implementation units or partitions.

### What is `import std;` (C++23, P2465R3)

C++23 standardizes the modular Standard Library. Instead of including dozens of standard headers, importing `std` makes the entire standard library available almost instantaneously through a precompiled Binary Module Interface (BMI).

```cpp
// modern_app.cpp
import std; // Provides std::vector, std::print, std::ranges, std::expected, etc.

int main() {
    std::vector<int> numbers = {5, 3, 1, 4, 2};
    std::ranges::sort(numbers);
    std::println("Sorted: {}", numbers);
    return 0;
}
```

### Difference between `import std;` and `import std.compat;`

| Feature | `import std;` | `import std.compat;` |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Namespace** | Everything in `namespace std` | `namespace std` + global C functions (`::printf`, `::memcpy`) |
| **Recommended for** | New C++23/C++26 codebases | Migrating legacy code with unqualified C library calls |
| **Standard** | C++23 | C++23 |

### Dealing with Standard Macros (`assert`, `errno`)

Modules **do not export macros**. Macro names from the preprocessor cannot cross module boundaries.

```cpp
// main.cpp
module; // Global module fragment for macros
#include <cassert>

export module my_service;
import std;

export void process(int value) {
    assert(value > 0); // Works because <cassert> is in global module fragment
    std::println("Value: {}", value);
}
```

### Compiler & Standard Support Matrix
- **MSVC**: Supported since Visual Studio 2022 17.5+ (`/std:c++latest` or `/std:c++20` with standard library modules enabled).
- **Clang**: Supported since Clang 17+ / Clang 18+ (`-fmodules -std=c++23`). Requires a **modularized libc++**: libstdc++ ships its `std` module for GCC, not for Clang, so a libstdc++-only host cannot build `import std;` with Clang. Install libc++ (e.g. `libc++-dev`/`libc++abi-dev`) and build the std module with Clang, or stay on GCC.
- **GCC**: Supported since GCC 14+ (`-std=c++23 -fmodules-ts`).
- **CMake**: Supported natively in CMake 3.28+ with Ninja generator (CMake 3.30+ can build the std module via `set(CMAKE_CXX_MODULE_STD ON)`).

### Toolchain conflict: BMI format vs clangd

Binary Module Interfaces are **compiler-specific**: GCC emits `.gcm`, Clang emits `.pcm`. A toolchain cannot read the other's BMIs. Consequently, if your build produces the `std` module with GCC but your editor/clangd runs Clang (or vice versa), module navigation and `import std;` resolution fail with `module_not_found` even though the build itself succeeds. Pick one toolchain end-to-end (or build the std module with Clang + libc++) so the same compiler produces both the BMIs and the clangd index. See the xmake `cxx-modules` recipe (`get_cpp_tooling_guide`) for per-toolchain setup.

### Architecture of Module Partitions

Module partitions allow splitting a large module across multiple files while presenting a single cohesive module interface to callers.

```
                   [Consumer: import engine;]
                              │
                              ▼
                   [export module engine;]  (Primary Interface)
                      ├── export import :types;     (Interface Partition)
                      └── export import :renderer;  (Interface Partition)
                               │           │
                    (internal) └── import :helpers; (Internal Partition)
```

### 1. Interface Partition (`export module <name>:<partition>;`)

Contributes to the public interface of the module:

```cpp
// engine_types.cppm
export module engine:types;

export namespace engine {
    struct Vec3 {
        float x, y, z;
    };
}
```

### 2. Internal Partition (`module <name>:<partition>;`)

Visible only inside module `engine`. Cannot be exported to outside users:

```cpp
// engine_helpers.cppm
module engine:helpers; // NO 'export' before 'module'

namespace engine {
    void validate_hardware_acceleration();
}
```

### 3. Primary Module Interface Aggregator

Aggregates partitions into the public API:

```cpp
// engine.cppm
export module engine;

// Re-export interface partitions
export import :types;
export import :renderer;

// Import internal partition for own usage without re-exporting
import :helpers;

export namespace engine {
    void initialize() {
        validate_hardware_acceleration();
    }
}
```

### Preventing Cyclic Dependencies with Partitions
If Partition A needs Partition B, and Partition B needs Partition A:
1. Extract shared type definitions into a lower-level partition `module engine:common_types;`.
2. Both Partition A and Partition B import `:common_types`.
3. Keep the dependency graph strictly a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG).

### The Purpose of the Global Module Fragment (GMF)

The global module fragment exists to provide backward compatibility with traditional C and C++ header files that rely on the preprocessor or are not modularized.

```cpp
// Correct pattern for legacy headers
module; // 1. Start of Global Module Fragment

#include <openssl/ssl.h> // Legacy C header with extensive macro definitions
#include <zlib.h>
#define BUFFER_SIZE 4096

export module secure_channel; // 2. Start of Module Purview

import std; // Modular imports go here in the purview

export namespace net {
    class TlsSocket {
    public:
        explicit TlsSocket();
        ~TlsSocket();
        void write(std::span<const std::byte> data);
    private:
        SSL* ssl_handle_{nullptr};
    };
}
```

### Why `#include` in Module Purview is an Anti-Pattern

```cpp
// BAD ANTI-PATTERN:
export module bad_example;
#include <windows.h> // DISASTER: All macros (min, max, ERROR) pollute the module purview!

export void run() {}
```

**Consequences of putting `#include` inside purview:**
1. Macro collisions across translations units.
2. Header contents get compiled into the module's BMI, bloating BMI size.
3. Can cause silent ODR conflicts with other modules importing the same header.

### Types of Linkage in Modern C++

1. **External linkage**: Declared with `export`. Usable from any translation unit that imports the module.
2. **Module linkage**: Declared in a module without `export` and without `static` or anonymous namespace. Visible to all units and partitions of the **same module**, but completely inaccessible outside.
3. **Internal linkage**: Declared with `static` or in an anonymous namespace. Visible only within the current translation unit.

```cpp
export module security;

// 1. External linkage: Accessible to all importers
export void authenticate(std::string_view user);

// 2. Module linkage: Accessible to security implementation units & partitions only
void internal_crypto_hash(std::span<const std::byte> data);

// 3. Internal linkage: Accessible only within security.cppm
namespace {
    constexpr int SALT_ROUNDS = 12;
}
```

### The 'Inline' Revolution with Modules
In classic C++, defining a non-template function in a header required `inline` to prevent the linker from throwing `multiple definition of 'foo'` (ODR violation).

In C++20 modules:
- Every module interface is compiled **once** into a Binary Module Interface (BMI).
- Functions defined in module purviews are **not duplicate definitions**.
- You only need `inline` if you want the compiler to consider inlining across module boundaries for performance.

### Strategy 1: The Module Façade (Easiest & Fastest)

Wrap existing, tested header files in a module interface. This provides instant compilation speedups without rewriting existing source code.

```cpp
// mylib.cppm
module;
#include "mylib/matrix.h"
#include "mylib/vector.h"
#include "mylib/solver.h"

export module mylib;

export namespace mylib {
    using mylib::Matrix;
    using mylib::Vector;
    using mylib::solve_linear_system;
}
```

### Strategy 2: Dual-Mode Header (For Shared Libraries)

When authoring a library that must support both C++17 (headers) and C++20 (modules):

```cpp
// mylib_api.h
#ifdef MYLIB_BUILD_MODULE
  #define MYLIB_EXPORT export
#else
  #define MYLIB_EXPORT
#endif

// mylib/types.h
MYLIB_EXPORT namespace mylib {
    struct Config {
        int timeout_ms;
    };
}
```

In the module interface file:
```cpp
// mylib.cppm
module;
#define MYLIB_BUILD_MODULE
#include "mylib/types.h"

export module mylib;
```

### Recommended Migration Order
1. **Level 0 (Leaf utilities)**: Math, formatting, string helpers, error types.
2. **Level 1 (Data structures & Core algorithms)**: Models, collections, business types.
3. **Level 2 (Services & Subsystems)**: Network, database, rendering.
4. **Level 3 (Application / Entrypoint)**: Main executable, CLI dispatch.

### 1. Leaky Reachability Anti-Pattern

```cpp
// BAD:
export module store;

class DatabaseConnection { /* not exported */ };

export DatabaseConnection& get_connection();
```

**The Problem**: A caller can call `auto& conn = get_connection();`, but `conn` has an unnameable type. Attempting to use methods or pass it to templates will trigger confusing compiler errors.

**The Fix**: If a type appears in the signature of an exported entity, export that type as well.

### 2. Importer Macro Independence Gotcha

In classic C++:
```cpp
// Caller
#define DEBUG_MODE 1
#include <engine.h> // engine.h behavior changes based on DEBUG_MODE!
```

In C++ Modules:
```cpp
// Caller
#define DEBUG_MODE 1
import engine; // DOES NOTHING! engine BMI was already compiled in isolation!
```

**Best Practice**: Never use preprocessor flags to configure module behavior. Pass configuration via parameters, template arguments, or build-system configuration constants.

### C++26 Contracts and Modules (P2900)

C++26 introduces native Contracts (`pre`, `post`, `contract_assert`). When exported in a module interface, contract predicates are visible to callers for static analysis and runtime checking without leaking implementation details.

```cpp
// C++26 Module Interface with Contracts
export module account_service;

import std;

export namespace banking {
    class Account {
    public:
        void deposit(double amount)
            pre(amount > 0.0); // Contract precondition part of module interface

        [[nodiscard]] double balance() const noexcept;
    };
}
```

### C++26 Reflection (`^` reflexpr) with Modules (P2996)

Reflection enables inspecting types at compile-time without relying on preprocessor macros or external code-generation steps. Because modules isolate symbol visibility, reflection over module-exported types provides clean introspection without leaking internal implementation details.
